================================================================================
Where the facts come from
================================================================================

Every rule states one fact about how a server behaves, and each was established
from a primary source rather than from recollection. The source is recorded on
the rule, printed by `serverdoctor rules`, shown by `--verbose`, and listed in
docs/rules.md, so any claim this tool makes can be checked. The line numbers were
read on 2026-08-24 from the default branch of each repository.

  What the server prints when something breaks
    Read from PaperMC/Paper: WatchdogThread.java (the hang banner, the 300-second
    substitution for a non-positive timeout-time, and the disable.watchdog
    property), AsyncCatcher.java (the failed main-thread check and the
    "Asynchronous ...!" exception), SimplePluginManager.java (event blame, load
    failures, ambiguous plugin names, the deprecation nag), CraftMagicNumbers.java
    (unsupported and missing api-version), and the patch files for
    MinecraftServer, DedicatedServer and Main (the deleted overload warning, the
    port-bind failure, and the EULA line logged at INFO).

  Folia's refusal to load a plugin
    Read from PaperMC/Folia: the folia-supported descriptor key and the exact
    comparison against the literal "true", plus the two refusal messages in the
    region-threading patch.

  Configuration defaults
    Read from the fields that declare them in Paper's own configuration classes,
    GlobalConfiguration.java and WorldConfiguration.java, and from SpigotConfig
    .java for the spigot.yml keys. No default is quoted from a tuning guide,
    because a guide can be a version behind and a field cannot.

  Worlds on disk
    Read from PaperMC/Paper: WorldFolderMigration.java, for the Data compound that
    carries the level data and for the two historic layouts the server migrates
    between (the CraftBukkit sibling folders and the vanilla DIM-1 / DIM1). The
    structural facts - a folder with region data and no level.dat, a zero-byte
    .mca, a datapack folder without pack.mcmeta - are read from the directory
    itself and the rules say so rather than citing behaviour that could not be
    sourced. The NBT reader is written from the format itself and produces only
    maps, lists and primitives.

  Identity, operators and permissions
    Read from PaperMC/Paper CraftServer.java: getOfflinePlayer, where an account's
    real id is only looked up when online mode is on and is otherwise created from
    the name that was typed, and loadCustomPermissions, where the permissions file
    is named by bukkit.yml settings.permissions-file, a missing file returns
    silently, and YAML that does not parse is logged once and the whole file
    skipped. The access lists themselves are read as the JSON the server writes.
    enforce-whitelist is printed as a fact and has no rule, because its behaviour
    lives in code that is not in Paper's repository and could not be verified.

  Crash reports
    The layout was taken from real reports rather than from documentation: the
    banner, Time and Description, the trace, then `-- Name --` sections whose
    bodies are tab-indented `Key: value` lines. `-- System Details --` carries the
    Minecraft and Java versions, the memory figures and the `JVM Flags: N total;`
    line the flags audit is then run over. Nothing is inferred from a trace beyond
    naming the first frame that belongs to neither the server, the JDK nor Netty.

  JVM flags
    The recommended set and the reasoning behind matching -Xms to -Xmx come from
    PaperMC/docs (BSD-2-Clause), paper/admin/how-to/aikars-flags.md. The removal
    of the Concurrent Mark Sweep collector is cited to JEP 363. Nothing in the
    flags audit treats a missing tuning option as a defect.

  Plugin provenance
    Modrinth's API v2, verified by request on 2026-08-24: a file lookup by SHA-1
    returns the version it belongs to, and the project endpoint carries the status,
    licence and last-updated timestamp used for the abandonment signals.

    Hangar's API v1, verified by request on 2026-08-24 and asked only when a digest
    lookup has already missed: slug lookup is case-insensitive, an unknown slug
    answers 404, the versions listing is newest first and clamps its page size to
    25, and each version carries downloads.<platform>.fileInfo.sha256Hash - the
    digest of the file Hangar itself serves. Verified end to end against a real
    published build, whose downloaded bytes hashed to the value the API reports.
    Hangar has no endpoint that takes a digest, so that lookup runs by name, which
    is a weaker claim than a hash and is worded as one wherever it is reported.

    Requests to both carry a User-Agent naming this tool, as those APIs ask.

  Region file layout
    Read from PaperMC/Paper: the header is the first two 4 KiB sectors and each of
    the 1024 locations packs a sector offset and a length in sectors, per
    paper-server/patches/features/0026-Attempt-to-recalculate-regionfile-header-
    if-it-is-co.patch, which exists because Paper repairs that header when it is
    corrupt. A location whose sector overlaps the header is the warning in
    patches/sources/net/minecraft/world/level/chunk/storage/RegionFile.java.patch.
    A length of 255 sectors is Spigot's marker for a chunk stored oversized, whose
    real length lives in the chunk's own prefix, so it is deliberately not
    bounds-checked - treating it as a size would report healthy files as damaged.
    Vanilla's own padding behaviour is not in Paper's repository and no rule
    depends on it.

  Model Context Protocol
    Message shapes, the newline-delimited stdio framing rule and the error codes
    follow the specification at modelcontextprotocol/modelcontextprotocol.

  Class file versions
    Fixed by the JVM specification: major 52 is Java 8 and each release adds one.

No Minecraft, Mojang, Paper, CraftBukkit, Bukkit or Folia code is included in,
derived into, or distributed with this project, and no game data is bundled. The
rule table quotes short diagnostic strings only as far as needed to recognise
them in a log, so that its claims stay auditable against their sources.

